  "query_preview": "..An upper level low will continue to shift down the\nPacific Coast through later Thursday into Friday and then\ngradually eject across the Great Basin through Saturday.\nDiffluent southwest flow will remain in place, with modest\nmoisture transport through at least later Thursday. Embedded in\nthis southwest flow will be a multitude of weak shortwave\ntroughs or remnant MCVs. This, combined with PWATs in the 96-99th\npercentile, and broad ascent will support periods of convection\nwith very heavy rainfall, especially through Friday morning. \n\nEarlier thunderstorms, with occasional embedded supercells\nproduced rainfall rates as high as 0.50 inches in 20 minutes,\ncausing multiple flash floods in the Wahweap, lower Paria\ndrainages as well as various slot canyons in the San Rafael Swell\nincluding Little Wildhorse north of Goblin Valley State Park.\n\nCurrent model trends indicate another subtle shortwave ejecting\nacross northern Arizona/southern Nevada and southwest Utah will\naid in the development of convection overnight through about 6 AM\nthis morning across lower Washington County, Iron County and\nwestern Kane and Garfield Counties. Rainfall rates in excess of\n0.25 inches an hour will be possible with this convection, with\naround a 10% chance of rainfall rates in excess of 0.50 inches per\nhour. A Flood Watch for flash flooding begins at midnight tonight\nand continues through Thursday morning for Zion National Park and\npoints east. No changes have been made to the going Watch.\n\nThe Storm Prediction Center has outlooked portions of northern\nUtah for a marginal risk for severe thunderstorms, particularly\nfor Wednesday morning into early Wednesday afternoon. A few strong\nthunderstorms will be possible along the periphery of the deeper\nmoisture in this area. \n\nAfternoon convection will kick off across the region, with a\nmultitude of CAMS showing very widespread heavy rain across most\nof southern Utah after about 00Z. This may require the expansion\nof the Flood Watch into lower Washington, Iron and Beaver Counties\nfor late Wednesday night into Thursday afternoon. Something to\nmonitor for future adjustments if CAMS remain consistent. \n\nIt is not recommended to have plans into slot canyons, normally\ndry washes, and other areas prone to flash flooding over the next\nseveral days. Nearly all National Park and Rec areas are currently\nat Probable for the next several days in the flash flood potential\nproduct.",
